`var` is function-scoped, so there is one `i` shared by all three callbacks. `setTimeout` callbacks run after the loop finishes, when `i` has already been incremented to 3, so each callback reads 3.

Fixes:

```js
// let creates a fresh binding per iteration
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  setTimeout(() => console.log(i), 0);
}

// or, with var, capture the value via an IIFE
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) {
  ((j) => setTimeout(() => console.log(j), 0))(i);
}
```
